\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Coordinate covariance of the sector product law}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{\citet{Proposition B.55, p. 505}{friedli2017statistical}
uses covariance as its positive parameter. Choose orthonormal bases
of #{H_+} and #{H_-}, indexed by #{I_+} and #{I_-}, and concatenate
them. The coefficient map
#{C:H\to\mathbb R^{I_+\sqcup I_-}} is an isometry. Write
#{R=CQC^{-1}} and set #{\Sigma=R^{-1}}. Its eigenvalues are positive,
#{\Sigma^{-1}=R} and #{\det\Sigma=(\det R)^{-1}}, so its
normalized density is exactly #{Z_Q^{-1}w_Q}. The
[inverse-precision covariance theorem](fdip-000B) identifies
#{\Sigma} as the array of the covariance. The proof of
\ref{fdip-000M} uses densities, not an inference from zero covariance
for an unspecified law.}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{OrthonormalBasis.measurePreserving_repr}
supports passage to orthonormal coordinates. Index coordinates by the
disjoint union of orthonormal bases of #{H_+} and #{H_-}. Then
#{\Sigma=\operatorname{diag}(R_+^{-1},R_-^{-1})}, where
#{R_\pm=C_\pm Q_\pm C_\pm^{-1}}, is positive definite, hence positive
semidefinite, with zero cross block and mean zero. After the density
identification, TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.indepFun_sumEquivProd_multivariateGaussian_iff}
gives the corresponding coordinate independence. It concerns this
Gaussian covariance, not an arbitrary algebraic splitting or Schur
fiber integral.}
